





{"id":"d5523653-92dc-450d-9d34-d8bc29d3ddc9","object":"chat.completion","created":1786441941,"model":"deepseek-v4-pro","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"record_id\": \"CA12_CORE_031\",\n  \"decisions\": [\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_01\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_03\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_01\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 6,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_02\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_02\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_04\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 22,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_03\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_01\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_03\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 7,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_04\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_03\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_08\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 9,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_05\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_01\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_02\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 11,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_06\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_03\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_09\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 17,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    }\n  ]\n}","reasoning_content":"We are given a public task for a UAV integrated-repair optimization. The task requires us to output a JSON with a list of decisions for each mission, exactly following the schema. We need to respect fixed UAV assignment and mission order, route compatibility, release/due windows, incoming deadhead, charging, SOC/reserve, corridor/pad/charger capacity, closures, and all intervention overrides. Optimize lexicographically: urgent holds, total holds, repair distance, weighted tardiness, delay plus charge wait, then energy.\n\nWe are given a baseline (candidate) and the instance with interventions. The baseline decisions are provided as a reference, but we need to produce a repair that corrects any issues. The task says: \"Return one complete repair covering every mission exactly once.\" So we need to check if the baseline is feasible under the interventions, and if not, adjust decisions (hold missions, change paths, departure slots, charge blocks) to make it feasible and optimize the lexicographic objectives.\n\nFirst, understand the instance structure:\n- fleet: 3 UAVs (UAV_01, UAV_02, UAV_03)\n- horizon_slots: 34 (slots from 0 to 33? slot_min is 5, so maybe slots 5..33? Actually, horizon_slots 34 means 34 slots, slot numbers? The available_slot for UAVs is 6. So earliest start is slot 6. The release slots for missions: MISSION_01: 6, MISSION_02: 22, MISSION_03: 6, MISSION_04: 9, MISSION_05: 10, MISSION_06: 17. So the earliest slot any mission can depart is its release slot. The due slots: MISSION_01: 29, MISSION_02: 29, MISSION_03: 8, MISSION_04: 12, MISSION_05: 24, MISSION_06: 26.\n\nThe assignment is fixed UAV and fixed order. That means each UAV has a sequence of missions assigned to it, and we cannot change the UAV. The given missions already have uav_id fields (from the baseline? Actually, the missions list has uav_id directly: MISSION_01: UAV_03, MISSION_02: UAV_02, MISSION_03: UAV_01, MISSION_04: UAV_03, MISSION_05: UAV_01, MISSION_06: UAV_03). So the UAV assignments are fixed. Moreover, \"FIXED_UAV_ASSIGNMENT_AND_FIXED_UAV_ORDER\" means the order of missions per UAV is fixed. For UAV_01, missions: MISSION_03 (sequence_index 0), then MISSION_05 (sequence_index 1). For UAV_02: MISSION_02 (seq 0). For UAV_03: MISSION_01 (seq 0), MISSION_04 (seq 1), MISSION_06 (seq 2). So we must serve them in that order for each UAV.\n\nWe need to decide for each mission: held (true/false), path_id (if not held), depart_slot (if not held), charge_blocks (post-service charge blocks, 0..3). If held, path_id and depart_slot are null, charge_blocks 0.\n\nThe normative SOC event order: For every served mission:\nincoming deadhead -> service flight -> reserve check -> destination turnaround -> post-service charge\nSo when a UAV performs a mission, it first travels an incoming deadhead if needed (from its start location or previous mission destination to origin of this mission). Then the service flight (the mission's path). Then we check reserve (ensure SOC after subtracting incoming deadhead energy and service flight energy is >= reserve). Then destination turnaround (turnaround_slots: 1 slot). Then post-service charge (the charge_blocks we specify) at the destination vertiport. The charge energy becomes available after the charge blocks.\n\nFor the start of the first mission for a UAV, there is no incoming deadhead? The mission has incoming_deadhead field, and it is null for all missions. But the UAV has a start_vertiport \"VP_04\". And each mission has an origin_vertiport and an expected_incoming_origin_vertiport. For MISSION_01, origin_vertiport VP_04, expected_incoming_origin_vertiport VP_04. Since start_vertiport of UAV_03 is VP_04, there is no deadhead? Actually, the normative order says \"incoming deadhead\" - if the UAV is already at the origin, the deadhead distance is 0, energy 0, and takes 0 slots. So we just consider no deadhead. For subsequent missions, the UAV is at the destination of the previous mission after turnaround. So the deadhead from previous destination to next origin must be considered. However, the task specifies \"incoming_deadhead\" field in the mission is null, and there is a \"deadhead_contract\": \"FIXED_REGISTERED_INCOMING_DEADHEAD_PATH\". That might mean that the deadhead paths are precomputed and we need to use a specific deadhead? But looking at the missions, each has an expected_incoming_origin_vertiport. For MISSION_04, origin is VP_07, but previous UAV_03 mission MISSION_01 destination is VP_07. So if served, no deadhead. For MISSION_05, origin VP_07, previous UAV_01 mission MISSION_03 destination VP_07, so no deadhead. For MISSION_06, origin VP_02, previous UAV_03 mission MISSION_04 destination VP_02, so no deadhead. So in this instance, all UAVs start at VP_04, first missions origin VP_04, subsequent missions origin matches previous destination. So there are no deadhead legs needed. Good.\n\nNow, we need to check the feasibility of the baseline decisions given the interventions.\n\nInterventions:\n- initial_soc_overrides: [{\"initial_soc_kwh\":1.70086,\"uav_id\":\"UAV_01\"}] - so UAV_01 initial SOC changed from 2.75086 to 1.70086.\n- pad_capacity_overrides: [{\"capacity\":1,\"slot\":6,\"vertiport_id\":\"VP_04\"}] - pad capacity at VP_04 at slot 6 reduced to 1 (default is 2).\n- closed_corridor_slots: COR_07 closed from slot 9 to slot 32 inclusive (slots 9-32). So corridor COR_07 cannot be used during those slots.\n- No charger capacity overrides or corridor capacity overrides.\n\nWe need to produce a repair that is feasible with these interventions.\n\nLet's list baseline decisions:\n\n- MISSION_01: UAV_03, path PATH_01, depart_slot 6, charge_blocks 0. Path PATH_01: VP_04->VP_07, corridor COR_05, distance 1.08 km, travel_slots 2, energy 0.17368, wind_level 1. Depart slot 6, so flight slots 6-7? travel_slots 2 means occupies slots 6 and 7? Edge segment duration_slots 2, offset 0, so departure at slot 6, arrival at slot 6+2=8? Actually, normally if depart at slot d, the flight occupies slots d to d+travel_slots-1? The travel_slots is number of slot intervals. So if depart_slot=6, travel_slots=2, the flight is from slot 6 to slot 7 (arrival at the beginning of slot 8?). But we'll consider that the UAV is occupied and the corridor is used during those slots. Then after service flight, reserve check, turnaround 1 slot, post-service charge 0 blocks. So the UAV becomes available for next mission at the destination after turnaround. Turnaround slot duration: 1 slot. So if arrival at end of slot 7? Let's define: Depart at slot d, flight duration in slots = travel_slots. The flight ends at the end of slot (d + travel_slots - 1). Turnaround takes 1 slot, so the turnaround occupies slot (d + travel_slots), and then post-service charge starts. So the UAV can depart on the next mission at slot (d + travel_slots + turnaround_slots + charge_blocks) if no deadhead. So for MISSION_01: depart 6, travel 2 slots (6-7), turnaround slot 8, no charge. So UAV_03 available at VP_04? Wait, MISSION_01 destination is VP_07. So after turnaround, UAV_03 is at VP_07, available at slot 9.\n\n- MISSION_04: UAV_03, path PATH_07, depart_slot 9, charge_blocks 0. Path PATH_07: VP_07->VP_02, corridor COR_07, distance 1.0 km, travel_slots 2, energy 0.160815, wind_level 1. But COR_07 is closed slots 9-32. Depart slot 9, so flight slots 9-10. Slot 9 is within closure, so this path is not allowed. So this baseline is infeasible due to corridor closure. PATH_07 is incompatible. Alternative paths for MISSION_04: PATH_08 (corridor COR_08, travel_slots 1, energy 0.160815) and PATH_10 (corridor COR_01, travel_slots 4, energy 0.32163). All must be checked for closure. COR_08 closure: none, so allowed. COR_01 closure: none. Also need to check corridor capacity, pad capacity, etc. But baseline choice PATH_07 is infeasible due to closure. So we must change.\n\n- MISSION_06: UAV_03, path PATH_09 (COR_04, travel_slots 1, energy 0.313589), depart_slot 17, charge_blocks 0. \n- MISSION_03: UAV_01, path PATH_03 (COR_02, travel_slots 2, energy 0.150516), depart_slot 6, charge_blocks 0. \n- MISSION_05: UAV_01, path PATH_02 (COR_03, travel_slots 1, energy 0.100344), depart_slot 10, charge_blocks 0.\n- MISSION_02: UAV_02, path PATH_04 (COR_10, travel_slots 2, energy 0.113), depart_slot 22, charge_blocks 0.\n\nNow, check if PATH_03 for MISSION_03 is feasible. Depart slot 6, travel 2 slots (6-7), turnaround slot 8, available at VP_07 (destination) at slot 9. MISSION_05 depart slot 10 from VP_07, feasible. But need to check reserve and energy for UAV_01 with initial SOC override. UAV_01 initial SOC = 1.70086 (override). For MISSION_03: service flight energy 0.150516. So after service, SOC1 = 1.70086 - 0.150516 = 1.550344. Reserve 1.5, so SOC1 >= reserve, ok. Then charge_blocks 0, so no charge. Turnaround 1 slot, then available. For MISSION_05, depart slot 10, travel 1 slot, energy 0.100344. So SOC after MISSION_05 = 1.550344 - 0.100344 = 1.45. That is below reserve 1.5? Wait, reserve check occurs after the service flight of MISSION_05. Before MISSION_05, we have SOC from end of MISSION_03. No charging. So SOC before MISSION_05 is 1.550344. After MISSION_05 service flight, it becomes 1.45. Reserve is 1.5. So that would be below reserve, infeasible. So baseline for UAV_01 is infeasible due to SOC/reserve violation.\n\nWe need to adjust: either hold MISSION_05 or add charge blocks after MISSION_03 to increase SOC. After MISSION_03, we could have post-service charge at VP_07. If we set charge_blocks > 0 for MISSION_03, then SOC increases by charge_kwh_per_block * charge_blocks before MISSION_05. UAV_01 has charge_kwh_per_block = 1.2. So to get SOC after MISSION_05 above reserve, we need enough energy. Currently after MISSION_03, SOC = 1.550344. We need after MISSION_05, SOC >= 1.5. So energy needed for MISSION_05 is 0.100344, so starting SOC must be at least 1.600344. So we need to gain at least 1.600344 - 1.550344 = 0.05 kWh. One charge block gives 1.2 kWh, which is more than enough. But charging takes time: charge_blocks = 1 adds 1 slot. So then MISSION_05 depart slot would shift later. Also pad capacity at VP_07? Default pad capacity 2, no overrides. Charger capacity: default 2, but we need to occupy a charger during charge. If we add 1 charge block, turnaround ends at slot 9? Actually MISSION_03: depart 6, travel 2 (6-7), turnaround slot 8, then charge block(s) start after turnaround. So if charge_blocks = 1, charging occupies slot 9? That would push MISSION_05 depart slot to at least 10 (since charge ends after slot 9, available at slot 10). But baseline MISSION_05 depart_slot 10 would now be ok if charge ends before slot 10? Charge block duration: if we have 1 charge block, it occupies one slot. Turnaround takes slot 8, then charge slot 9. So UAV available at slot 10. So depart_slot 10 is feasible. Need to check pad and charger capacity at VP_07 at slot 9. Pad capacity 2, charger capacity 2. Only UAV_01 is charging? Possibly other UAVs might also be at VP_07. We'll need to check. Also need to check if MISSION_03's due slot is 8, which is before turnaround and charge. The due slot constraint: service flight must arrive before due slot? Or must depart? The due slot is the latest the mission should be completed? Usually, the service flight must be completed by the due slot, meaning arrival time (depart + travel_slots - 1) <= due_slot? Or maybe due slot is the slot by which the mission must be finished, so arrival at end of travel_slots slot <= due_slot. For MISSION_03, due_slot=8, travel_slots=2, depart=6 => arrival at end of slot 7, so ok. Changing charge blocks doesn't affect the service flight timing, only post-service. So MISSION_03 remains on time.\n\nNow, for MISSION_04 (UAV_03), baseline depart 9 with PATH_07 is infeasible due to closure. We need to choose another path and possibly adjust timing. UAV_03 sequence: MISSION_01 (depart 6, PATH_01, charge_blocks 0) -> arrival VP_07 at slot 8? Actually travel_slots 2, so flight 6-7, turnaround slot 8, available at VP_07 slot 9. MISSION_04 origin VP_07, so could depart as early as slot 9. But the path options: PATH_08 (COR_08, travel_slots 1, energy 0.160815), PATH_07 (closed), PATH_10 (COR_01, travel_slots 4, energy 0.32163). So we can choose PATH_08 or PATH_10. PATH_08 travel_slots 1, so if depart slot 9, arrival slot 9 (since travel_slots=1, it occupies only slot 9). Then turnaround slot 10, available at VP_02 at slot 11. Then MISSION_06 (origin VP_02) could depart as early as slot 11 or later, but release slot 17. Due slot 26. So PATH_08 would work. However, we need to check energy. UAV_03: initial SOC 10.67368, battery capacity 22.0, reserve 2.5, charge_kwh_per_block 0.95. MISSION_01 PATH_01 energy 0.17368. After MISSION_01, SOC = 10.67368 - 0.17368 = 10.5. Then MISSION_04 PATH_08 energy 0.160815. SOC after MISSION_04 = 10.5 - 0.160815 = 10.339185, >= reserve, ok. Then MISSION_06 PATH_09 energy 0.313589. SOC after = 10.025596, >= reserve. So no charging needed. But we must also check corridor capacities and pad capacities.\n\nCorridor closures: COR_07 closed 9-32. COR_08 is not closed. COR_01 not closed. Pad capacity at VP_04 at slot 6 is reduced to 1. Three UAVs start at VP_04, and two missions depart from VP_04 at slot 6: MISSION_01 (UAV_03) and MISSION_03 (UAV_01). That's two departures at slot 6, but pad capacity at VP_04 slot 6 is 1. This is a conflict! So baseline has two missions departing VP_04 at slot 6, but pad capacity override limits it to 1. So that is another infeasibility. We need to resolve pad collision at VP_04 slot 6. Possibly hold one mission, or shift departure slot.\n\nThus the baseline is infeasible due to: (1) pad capacity at VP_04 slot 6 (two missions departing), (2) UAV_01 reserve violation, (3) MISSION_04 path closure. We must repair.\n\nNow, objectives lexicographic: \n1. urgent holds - minimize number of urgent missions held (urgency: MISSION_03 and MISSION_04 are urgent, others routine). So we want to hold as few urgent missions as possible, ideally 0.\n2. total holds - minimize total number of missions held.\n3. repair distance - not sure what this means, maybe the sum of distances of selected paths? Or the \"repair\" specific distance? The term \"repair distance\" might be the total distance traveled across all missions, maybe minimizing the sum of path distances. But baseline has certain distances; we should try to minimize total distance.\n4. weighted tardiness - tardiness = max(0, completion slot - due_slot). Weighted how? Not specified, but likely each mission has a weight based on priority? Or equal weight. We'll assume minimize total tardiness.\n5. delay plus charge wait - unclear: maybe sum of (depart slot - release slot) plus waiting time for charging? Could be total delay from earliest possible departure plus charge time. We'll interpret as minimize sum of (depart_slot - release_slot) for non-held missions plus charge blocks? Not sure.\n6. energy - minimize total energy consumption across missions.\n\nWe want to find a feasible schedule that optimizes these in order.\n\nFirst, constraint resolution: avoid pad conflict at VP_04 slot 6 by adjusting departures. One of the two missions (MISSION_01 or MISSION_03) must depart at a different slot, or be held. Holding is allowed; held missions have no path/depart slot, count as held. We prefer not to hold urgent missions (MISSION_03 is urgent, MISSION_01 is routine). So maybe we hold MISSION_01? But holding MISSION_01 would be one hold (routine), while holding MISSION_03 would be urgent hold, which is worse. So we'd prefer to delay MISSION_01 rather than hold it, but delay might cause it to miss due slot? MISSION_01 due_slot=29, release 6, so delaying is ok as long as it can be completed before 29. But we need to check availability of UAV_03 after delay and the subsequent missions. Alternatively, we could change MISSION_03's path to PATH_05? PATH_05 has travel_slots 8, energy 1.50516, wind_level 1. That would require more energy but initial SOC 1.70086 wouldn't be enough? 1.70086 - 1.50516 = 0.1957 < reserve 1.5, so would need charging before? Actually, need reserve after service flight, so that would fail. So PATH_05 is infeasible for UAV_01 because it would draw SOC below reserve. So MISSION_03 must use PATH_03 or be held. PATH_03 travel 2, energy 0.150516. So MISSION_03 must depart VP_04. Pad capacity is 1 at slot 6, so only one can depart at slot 6. We need to resolve.\n\nOption: delay MISSION_03 to depart later? But if we delay MISSION_03, it must still arrive before due slot 8. Depart slot 6 gives arrival end of slot 7, due 8 ok. If we delay to depart slot 7, travel 2 slots (7-8), arrival end slot 8, that is exactly due slot 8, might be allowed if due slot means finish by the end of that slot. But pad capacity at VP_04 slot 7? No override, default pad capacity is 2. So slot 7 pad capacity is 2. So we could depart MISSION_03 at slot 7, and MISSION_01 at slot 6? But then UAV_01 would depart slot 7, and MISSION_05 would need to be scheduled later. But also we need to check MISSION_03 release slot 6, it can depart from 6 onward, so slot 7 is allowed. But then MISSION_05 release 10, we could still do it. However, MISSION_03's due slot is 8, arrival at end slot 8 is okay? The due slot: typically the mission must be completed by the end of that slot. So arrival at slot 8 (if flight ends at slot 8) is still within due. So depart slot 7 is feasible. But then we must check UAV_01's reserve and subsequent. And pad capacity at VP_04 slot 6 would have only MISSION_01 departing, capacity 1, ok.\n\nBut wait, what about UAV_02? MISSION_02 depart slot 22 from VP_04, no pad capacity issue at slot 6. So that's fine.\n\nSo possible repair: \n- UAV_01: MISSION_03 depart 7, PATH_03 (travel 2 slots 7-8), turnaround slot 9, charge_blocks needed? After MISSION_03, SOC = 1.70086 - 0.150516 = 1.550344. To do MISSION_05 (energy 0.100344), need starting SOC >= 1.600344 so after service SOC >= 1.5. So charge_blocks = (1.600344 - 1.550344)/1.2 = 0.05/1.2 = 0.0417, so 1 block sufficient. So set charge_blocks=1 for MISSION_03. Then charging takes slot 10 (since turn around slot 9, charge slot 10). UAV available at slot 11. MISSION_05 release 10, due 24, so depart slot 11 ok. MISSION_05 travel_slots 1 (slot 11), turnaround slot 12, available VP_06 slot 13. No further missions. Tardiness: MISSION_03 depart 7, release 6, delay=1. MISSION_05 depart 11, release 10, delay=1. Due slots: MISSION_03 arrival slot 8 (on time, tardiness 0), MISSION_05 arrival slot 11 (due 24, tardiness 0). So weighted tardiness 0. Total energy: 0.150516+0.100344=0.25086.\n\nAlternatively, we could hold MISSION_01 to resolve pad conflict, but that would be one hold (routine), while delaying MISSION_03 by 1 slot causes no holds, just 1 slot delay. The lexicographic first is urgent holds: 0 holds, so delaying is better than holding any urgent mission. Holding MISSION_01 is a routine hold, which is worse than 0 holds in total holds (0 vs 1). So total holds objective: 0 is better than 1. So we prefer delaying rather than holding any mission. So adjusting MISSION_03 depart to slot 7 seems good.\n\nNow, MISSION_01 (UAV_03) depart slot 6, PATH_01, charge_blocks 0, ok (pad capacity 1 at VP_04 slot 6, only this departure, fine). Check energy: initial SOC 10.67368, after MISSION_01 SOC 10.5. MISSION_04: we need to choose path and depart slot. Since MISSION_01 ends at VP_07, arrival? MISSION_01 depart 6, travel_slots 2, so flight 6-7, turnaround slot 8, available at VP_07 slot 9. MISSION_04 release 9, so can depart as early as 9. But we must pick a path. PATH_08 travel_slots 1, energy 0.160815, corridor COR_08, no closure. If depart 9, arrival slot 9, turnaround slot 10, available VP_02 slot 11. MISSION_06 release 17, so can be scheduled later. Check energy: after MISSION_04, SOC = 10.5 - 0.160815 = 10.339185. Then MISSION_06 (PATH_09, energy 0.313589) would bring to 10.025596, >=2.5 reserve, fine. No charging needed. However, need to verify corridor capacity at COR_08 slot 9. Default corridor capacity 2. Only MISSION_04 using it? Possibly other missions might use COR_08? No other missions. So ok. Pad capacity at VP_07 slot 9: default 2. UAV_03 is there for turnaround at slot 8 and then departure at slot 9. Also UAV_01 is at VP_07: after MISSION_03, UAV_01 arrives at VP_07? MISSION_03 destination VP_07, depart slot 7, travel 2 slots 7-8, arrival end of slot 8, turnaround slot 9. So UAV_01 occupies pad at VP_07 during slot 9? Turnaround is 1 slot, so it occupies pad slot 9. Meanwhile, UAV_03 departure at slot 9 also requires pad usage (departure). So both UAVs need pad at VP_07 at slot 9. Pad capacity is 2, so that's fine. Charger capacity: after MISSION_03, we plan charge_blocks=1 for UAV_01. That charge occurs in slot 10? Turnaround is slot 9, so charge block starts at slot 10 and occupies charger during slot 10. UAV_03 departs at slot 9, so no conflict.\n\nBut we must check MISSION_04 due slot 12. Depart 9, travel_slots 1, arrival slot 9, on time (tardiness 0). So ok.\n\nNow MISSION_06: release 17, due 26. UAV_03 available VP_02 at slot 11 after turnaround slot 10. So it can depart any time 11..? But need to respect due 26. Could depart slot 11 immediately? That would arrival slot 11 (travel_slots 1 for PATH_09), well before due. But we need to consider if there are any other constraints: pad capacity at VP_02, corridor capacity at COR_04, etc. Depart slot 11, travel 1 slot, arrival slot 11, turnaround slot 12. No other missions. So MISSION_06 could be scheduled at slot 11, but release slot 17, so earliest departure is 17. So we must respect release slot: cannot depart before release. So MISSION_06 must depart >=17. UAV_03 waits until slot 17 at VP_02. So depart slot 17, travel 1 slot, arrival 17, turnaround 18. All constraints fine. Tardiness 0.\n\nNow UAV_02: MISSION_02 depart slot 22, PATH_04 (COR_10, travel_slots 2, energy 0.113). Release 22, due 29. UAV_02 start VP_04, initial SOC 7.5, capacity 16, reserve 1.5. PATH_04 travel 2 slots (22-23), arrival end 23, turnaround slot 24. Energy: 7.5 - 0.113 = 7.387, >1.5 reserve, ok. No charge needed. No pad capacity issue at VP_04 slot 22 (default 2). Check if any other missions at VP_04 at slot 22? None. So fine. Due 29, completion arrival at end of slot 23, tardiness 0.\n\nNow, this schedule uses: UAV_01 MISSION_03 charge_blocks=1, depart 7; MISSION_05 depart 11. UAV_03 MISSION_01 depart 6, MISSION_04 depart 9 PATH_08, MISSION_06 depart 17 PATH_09; UAV_02 MISSION_02 depart 22 PATH_04. All missions served, no holds. So total holds 0, urgent holds 0. That satisfies the first two objectives optimally.\n\nNow, we need to check if any other constraints are violated: corridor capacities. For COR_05 (MISSION_01): slots 6-7. No other missions. COR_02 (MISSION_03): slots 7-8. No overlap. COR_08 (MISSION_04): slot 9. No others. COR_04 (MISSION_06): slot 17. No others. COR_10 (MISSION_02): slots 22-23. So all fine.\n\nCharger capacity: UAV_01 charges at VP_07 during slot 10. VP_07 charger capacity 2, only one charging, ok.\n\nPad capacity at VP_04 slot 6 is 1 (override), and we have only MISSION_01 departing, ok. VP_04 slot 7: MISSION_03 departing, and MISSION_01 already departed, so pad usage at slot 7: MISSION_03 departure. Capacity 2, ok. VP_04 slot 22: MISSION_02 departure, ok.\n\nWhat about the start of UAVs? UAV_01 initial SOC 1.70086, after initial SOC override, that's what we used. Good.\n\nNow check if there is any need for incoming deadhead? Already considered none.\n\nTurnaround: each mission after service flight has turnaround 1 slot. We accounted for that.\n\nNow, the \"repair distance\" objective: the baseline decisions had distances for the selected paths. Our repair changes MISSION_03 path from PATH_03 (distance 1.2 km) to still PATH_03? Actually we kept PATH_03 for MISSION_03. Baseline had PATH_03. So same distance. MISSION_04 changed from PATH_07 (distance 1.0 km) to PATH_08. PATH_08 distance? Looking at the path options: PATH_08: corridor COR_08, distance_km 1.0, same as PATH_07. So distance unchanged. Other missions unchanged. So total repair distance = original baseline distance sum? Baseline distances: PATH_01: 1.08, PATH_04: 1.0, PATH_03: 1.2, PATH_07: 1.0, PATH_02: 0.8, PATH_09: 1.95. Sum = 1.08+1.0+1.2+1.0+0.8+1.95 = 7.03. Our repair: PATH_01:1.08, PATH_04:1.0, PATH_03:1.2, PATH_08:1.0, PATH_02:0.8, PATH_09:1.95, same sum 7.03. So no change.\n\nWeighted tardiness: baseline